Context (pp. 22-33 of the 1986 'Analysis situs' notes): Grothendieck axiomatizes 'magasins'. A magasin is a set M of 'figures' with a part-of order <=, an order written with a small circle that reads as 'is interior to' (written ≪ below), and a relation |o| meaning 'have disjoint interiors' (non-overlapping). On p. 27 he proposed a notion of morphism, an 'application combinatoire'. This page gives examples meant to show that notion is 'not too idiotic'.

Example 2. Take a magasin M whose figures are built from a set L of atoms (cells). Consider phi: M -> Fig(L), X |-> molecules(X), the set of elementary pieces making up X. The target Fig(L) is the set-theoretic magasin of figures on L, as allowed by the set-theoretic Fig construction. Assume condition Mag L 1: for every X, atoms(X)° (atoms(X) minus its boundary) is nonempty, i.e. every figure has nonempty interior. Then:
- the support of molecules(X) is atoms(X);
- its interior is atoms(X)°;
- molecules(X) |o| molecules(Y) iff atoms(X)° ∩ atoms(Y)° = ∅.
Hence X |o| Y implies phi(X) |o| phi(Y), so phi preserves disjointness of interiors and is a combinatorial map.

Example 3 (begun). A surjective map of sets f: E' -> E induces, by inverse image, an injective combinatorial map of set-theoretic magasins Fig(E) -> Fig(E'). This is a contravariant, Stone-duality-like behaviour.

The next page then introduces a support function M -> P(P) into subsets of a point set, with axioms:
- monotone for ≪;
- interiors are nonempty;
- |o| iff the interiors are disjoint;
- interiors separate points.
That amounts to a representation of the abstract magasin by point sets.
